--- ANSWER ---
Your explanation is mostly right, but I would tighten it in two places:

- `(a)` is highly likely, though the source need not specifically be a `BooleanConstant`.
- For `(b)`, a nonconstant source is much more likely than an exotic numeric constant because your earlier topology already identifies `# of Auto-Reset` as the upstream value.

1. Ranking the missing feeders

1. **`Or.y` and `And.y`: Boolean source ??very high confidence.**

   A `DigitalNumericConstant` cannot directly be the Boolean source unless an intervening conversion/comparison node exists; LabVIEW wires are typed, although compatible numeric types can be coerced. [NI?�s wiring documentation](https://www.ni.com/docs/en-AS/bundle/labview/page/using-wires-to-link-block-diagram-objects.html)

   Most likely source classes:

   - `BooleanConstant`
   - `ControlTerminal` from a Boolean control
   - `Local`, `Global`, or shared-variable read
   - `Property` output, especially `Value`
   - `Function`, `Comparison`, `CompoundArithmetic`, `SubVI`, or Boolean Case output
   - tunnel, shift register, or feedback-node output

   Thus `(a)` is correct as ?�not a digital numeric constant,??but **`BooleanConstant` is only one candidate**.

2. **`Equal?.y`: runtime numeric source ??high confidence.**

   Since the measured chain says it is the `# of Auto-Reset` property value, the primary hypothesis should be:

   - `Property` node output, possibly transported through a tunnel; or
   - a `Local`/`ControlTerminal` if the census attribution was one hop removed.

   Exotic constants??EnumConstant`, `RingConstant`, fixed-point, refnum-like numeric constants, polymorphic/typedef-backed constants?�remain possible, but rank below the already observed property-dataflow explanation.

   A typedef does not by itself establish a distinct scripting class, so ?�typedef constant??is not yet a useful positive hypothesis. Measure its actual `Class Name`.

2. Does Traverse include subclasses?

**Yes: use `Class Name = Constant` to obtain the Constant class and its descendants.** The strongest directly relevant NI-hosted evidence is an NI Community answer recommending exactly three Traverse calls??SubVI`, `Constant`, and `Control`?�for finding all linkable objects. That recommendation only works if the filter includes descendants. The same discussion says `GObject` retrieves all diagram objects. [NI Community: Traverse for GObjects](https://forums.ni.com/t5/LabVIEW/Scripting-Reference-all-objects-traverse/td-p/3811133)

This also attacks part of `(b)`: if you traverse `Constant`, any genuine descendant constant should appear in that pass even if it was missed by `DigitalNumericConstant`.

Important distinction:

- The filter determines which runtime classes qualify.
- `Traverse for GObjects.vi` still returns **GObject-typed references**, not a statically `Constant[]` wire. A downcast/typed chain is still needed for class-specific properties.

Recommended cheap census:

1. Traverse `Constant`.
2. Report `Class Name`, UID, `Constant.Terminal`, connected-wire UID.
3. Only then dispatch values to class-typed readers.

3. Base `Constant.Terminal` and `Constant.Value`

**`Constant.Terminal` `634AC04` is a base-`Constant` property and therefore applies to every actual Constant descendant.** A single Constant-typed navigation op should be able to report:

`Constant.Terminal ??Terminal.Connected Wire ??Wire.UID`

for `BooleanConstant`, `StringConstant`, numeric subclasses, enum/ring constants, and other Constant descendants.

**Do not generalize the same way for the value.** Your measured LabVIEW 2026 behavior is decisive:

- base-typed `Constant.Value` returned a void variant for Boolean and numeric constants;
- `StringConstant` happened to carry data;
- the `DigitalNumericConstant`-typed property node returned the numeric value correctly.

Therefore the safe design is:

- base `Constant` op for identity, class, terminal and wire;
- per-concrete-class typed op for `Value`.

Treat the base `Value` result as unusable unless independently validated for that concrete class. NI?�s navigation example likewise downcasts an owner to `StringConstant` before accessing its class-specific value. [NI: Navigating from a Wire to a Node](https://www.ni.com/docs/en-US/bundle/labview/page/navigating-from-a-wire-to-a-node-using-vi-scripting.html)

4. Cheapest source-object lookup from a wire UID

Use the wire directly; do not search all constants or nodes again:

```text
Wire
  ??Wire.Terminals[]             6371003, output "Terms[]"
  ??Index Array[0]
  ??Terminal.Owner              6327806, output "Owner"
  ??Generic.Class Name          6327803, output "ClassName"
  ??GObject.UID                 632A813, output "UID"
```

NI specifies that index 0 of `Wire.Terminals[]` is the source when one exists; sinks begin at index 1 in unspecified order. It also explicitly prescribes `Terminal.Owner` to navigate from that terminal to the connected object. [NI navigation procedure](https://www.ni.com/docs/en-US/bundle/labview/page/navigating-from-a-wire-to-a-node-using-vi-scripting.html), [NI `Wire.Terminals[]`](https://www.ni.com/docs/en-US/bundle/labview-api-ref/page/properties-and-methods/vi-server/generic/gobject/wire/terminals.html)

Exact property-node output names:

| Property | ID | Output short name |
|---|---:|---|
| `Wire.Terminals[]` | `6371003` | `Terms[]` |
| `Generic.Owner` / `Terminal.Owner` | `6327806` | `Owner` |
| `Generic.Class Name` | `6327803` | `ClassName` |
| `GObject.UID` | `632A813` | `UID` |
| `Node.Label` | `6359001` | `Label` |
| `Text.Text` | `632D800` | `Text` |

`Owner` returns a Generic/GObject reference, so `ClassName` and `UID` are the cheapest cast-free identity. NI confirms both that `Owner` returns a Generic reference and that its short name is `Owner`. [NI `Owner`](https://www.ni.com/docs/en-US/bundle/labview-api-ref/page/properties-and-methods/vi-server/generic/owner2.html)

Then specialize by reported class:

- ordinary node terminal ??owner should be `Property`, `Local`, `Comparison`, `Function`, `SubVI`, etc.;
- `ControlTerminal` is a caveat: its owner can be the containing diagram rather than the front-panel control. In that case, identify the terminal itself by class/name/UID and join it to the existing panel-wiring census, rather than trusting `Owner` to return the control.

`Node.Label ??Text.Text` can add a human-readable name, but UID + concrete class should remain identity.

5. Can the 180-read scan miss a real feeder?

**Yes?�structure boundaries are the leading failure mode. Ordinary visual wire segments are not.**

- Bends and straight visual segments are parts of one wire object; they should not acquire separate UIDs merely because the drawing has several segments. NI distinguishes segments/branches graphically but models the wire as one source with potentially many sinks. [NI wire structure](https://www.ni.com/docs/en-AS/bundle/labview/page/using-wires-to-link-block-diagram-objects.html)
- Fan-out is not a problem: `Wire.Terminals[]` represents the source plus all sinks. NI says the source is first and other terminals are unordered. [NI `Terminals[]`](https://www.ni.com/docs/en-US/bundle/labview-api-ref/page/properties-and-methods/vi-server/generic/gobject/wire/terminals.html)
- **A tunnel is a problem:** the wires on the two sides of a structure boundary are separate wire objects and can have different UIDs. A constant may therefore report the outer wire while your selector census recorded the inner wire, or vice versa.
- Nested Case structures strengthen that risk: a tunnel may have a distinct inside wire for each subdiagram/case.
- Shift registers and feedback paths likewise separate the incoming, body, and outgoing nets.
- A constant wired to a tunnel can therefore be present in all 180 reads yet fail an exact UID equality test against the final selector wire.
- `wire = 0` plus error 1055 is consistent with an unwired terminal, but ensure errors are reset per element; otherwise an expected unwired constant could poison later iterations if the op?�s error dataflow is serialized.

The cheapest decisive next check is therefore:

1. Traverse `Constant` once and report class/UID/direct wire.
2. For each missing selector wire, use `Wire.Terminals[][0] ??Owner`.
3. If the owner is a tunnel/structure boundary, walk outward through that tunnel?�s inside/outside terminal properties and compare UIDs on both sides.
4. Only build Boolean/value readers after the source owner/class is known.

That will distinguish ?�wrong constant class??from ?�not a constant??and ?�same dataflow, different tunnel-side wire UID??without another 180-value scan.
